  "locus": "Theses theologicae De Veritate bonorum operum quae per Christi gratiam fiunt a Regenitis, I-XVIII (a bad tree cannot bear good fruit and all are bad trees by nature (Matt. 7; Gen. 6); Christ's grace makes them good trees, new creatures; the renewed truly do good works (Eph. 2:10; Titus 2:14; John 15); God judges them right and counts them as righteousness (1 Kings 15 and 14; Ps. 106; Deut. 30; Ps. 119; 2 Kings 23); without them no salvation (Heb. 12:14; Matt. 5, 7; John 15); they are not sins and differ from sins in kind and from the fine deeds of unbelievers; yet they are not perfect: lighter sins cling to them, and the acts themselves fall short of the law strictly taken; the objection that imperfect works are simply bad answered by the distinction between a defect of kind and a defect of degree).",
